+**Mixed fat-object scope (§§9–10, §12.3; K88).**
+
+A mixed fat object retains an ordinary native host binary and embeds ISA
+objects for marked device modules and their linked device closures (North
+Star §§2, 3.3, 4.4). The versioned §10.1 mixed-launch/1 transport binds
+native launch sites to selected device CC entries without replacing the
+§9.2 identity of any ISA object. Its counted Container/Call bytes and live
+annex have the following separate associations:
+
+| Association | Transport and required observation |
+|---|---|
+| container/root | Container.roots in canonical id order; selected root_index binds host_binding, exported CC entry, object_hash, interface_hash and closure_hash to the actual loaded object. Other native entry points are outside this inventory. |
+| target/certificate | Root.target_hash selects that program's target or null for a target-free load; certificates carry exact bytes and their decoded-byte SHA-256. Each certificate's own root, reached linked owner records, invocation/partition, schedule and complete contexts are independently checked. [] denotes that certificate's device root. |
+| program/token | The live annex binds the selected provider instance and actual program to the root binding and returned runs. Host tokens belong to that program; device mints also retain defining-frame/run ownership and revocation. No pointer or process-local handle id is serialized in a sidecar or certificate. |
+| tape/run | Each run retains its creating binding and provider/program/run pair through effects, wait/reply, reversal and close. Device tapes cover device R effects, not native process/libc activity. |
+| image/handle | Call.images transports complete §12.3 ImageLaunch objects; each launch_copy use additionally establishes its actual fresh factor, packing, resolved context and live handle association. Static manifest validity alone is conditional evidence. |
+| independent readers | Each checker/reader validates its own supported schemas and complete bindings; driver negotiation, successful mixed bind and provider advertisements cannot substitute for its checks. |
+| native comparison | Compare the corresponding materialised marked entry and complete materialised callee closure under the prescribed runtimes, arithmetic libraries and recorded configuration. Native main, argv/envp and host-only libc need no device frame, alias or certificate entries. |
+
+The copied-argument/entropy/image transport and live lifecycle are defined
+in §10.1. General host-memory-by-reference remains explicit boundary work:
+address/layout mapping, range/liveness, aliasing, ownership/synchronisation,
+writeback and reversal require a further versioned contract preserving
+dialect §§6.6, 8.5, 19.4–19.5. Missing mechanics are reported as deployment
+gaps before dispatch, never grounds for whole-binary lowering, source
+rejection, alias erasure or omitted mixed execution.
+
+**Fixed-image mixed-object binding (K85; §§9–10).**
+
+A native binary may carry VM objects only for marked
+modules. The host launch manifest selects a VM entry and records the
+classical image preparation/copy into its in-region, with resolved shape
+and raw-byte hash. The certificate inventory is that entry's selected VM
+closure and reached contexts, not the whole native executable. Image
+preparation may come from host database construction or a declared
+snapshot. Host bytes are bound to device cells using the declared packing,
+not host pointer identity. Unmarked main, argv/envp, libc and unrelated
+code remain native. The native comparison invokes the corresponding
+materialized marked entry over the same image and varying domain; it
+need not treat the entire binary as the oracle. Runtime shims and build
+configuration are recorded as required by the North Star.
+
+VM objects contain the selected marked entries and their required transitive device closures. Code outside those closures remains native; shared callees may have both host and device variants. Native comparison uses the materialised entry and its entire materialised callee closure with the North Star §3.2 runtimes and arithmetic libraries.
+
+**Process-domain and preparation bindings (K85).**
+
+The optional numerical/channel `domain` is the closed
+`bennett.process-domain/1` production in §12.1's payload rows. Its
+fixed_regions entries use exactly §12.2's name, A, W, little-endian raw image
+and SHA-256 encodings, with nonnegative integer A and positive W under §9.1.
+The certificate producer MUST list them in interface mem-slot order and the
+checker MUST resolve and validate them against that subject, rejecting a
+duplicate, mut-role region, bad shape, nonzero padding or stale raw-byte hash.
+All components other than those fixed in-role cells remain unrestricted,
+including inherited controls, arbitrary initial target/fault, borrowed
+systems and all mut-role cells. No replay omission-at-zero rule applies to
+this process production. Absent domain and an explicit domain with an empty
+fixed_regions array both mean the full original D. Additional fields, including
+bundle slices, are malformed in this version, not ignored restrictions.
+An unsupported process-domain version leaves the claim Unverified; the reader
+never interprets it as an empty or unrestricted domain.
+
+For each context, remove the fixed cell bits from the ordered input basis;
+J_I inserts their declared basis values at those same positions, together
+with the prescribed workspace/local initialisations. Remaining input bits
+retain their relative allocation order. Output retains the complete output
+interface, borrowed systems and all workspace/local outputs required to
+observe their restoration; it does not project out the fixed cells or hide
+leakage. Let d_in be the dimension after insertion inputs are removed and
+d_out the complete output dimension. For channel witnesses the input-first
+Choi row index is i*d_out+a (input i, output a); columns use j*d_out+b.
+Tr_out(Y)[i,j] is sum_a Y[i*d_out+a,j*d_out+a]. For numerical claims use the
+rectangular `(U−V)J_I` before taking the Frobenius bound; multiplication by
+V† is only the unrestricted-unitary alternative already in §12.1. In the
+strict-feasibility padding recipe, the trace factor d is d_out and the
+slack identity is I_d_in. These rules encode reduced-input/complete-output
+claims without discarding coherent leakage, relative phase or external
+references.
+
+A successful fixed-image process bound is conditional on I and is reported
+as such with its metric, bound, subject, claim index and ordered resolved
+image bindings. It is neither an unrestricted bound nor an exact permutation
+proof. In particular a nonzero process-error bound does not prove exact image
+restoration. Where an executable use requires preparation, the checker MUST
+report its applicability separately as established or unestablished; without
+a checked attachment below the bound/theorem remains conditional. This also
+applies to §12.2's proof_relative_to_image. Existing reference-interpreter
+Unverified outcomes for unavailable target/interval checks remain Unverified;
+adding domain metadata or a preparation descriptor cannot upgrade them.
